CAT = "cisaGovernance"; SEG = "GOV"
Q = [
("Who holds ultimate accountability for the governance of enterprise IT?",
 "The board of directors", ["The chief information officer", "The IT steering committee members", "The head of internal audit"],
 "Governance is a board responsibility: the board sets direction and oversees performance, while executive management runs IT day to day."),
("What is the main role of an IT strategy committee?",
 "To advise the board on IT strategy", ["To approve each project change request", "To run daily IT service operations", "To review individual firewall rules"],
 "An IT strategy committee works at board level on strategic IT issues. An IT steering committee, at management level, prioritizes and oversees projects and resources."),
("Which is the best evidence that IT is aligned with the business?",
 "IT plans derive from business plans", ["IT spending rose faster than last year", "The help desk closes most tickets quickly", "IT staff hold many certifications"],
 "Alignment means IT objectives come from business objectives. Efficient operations or skilled staff do not show that IT is working on what the business needs."),
("What does an IT balanced scorecard add to purely financial measures?",
 "Customer, process and growth views", ["A ranking of IT staff salaries", "A full list of every software license held", "Detailed project cost codes"],
 "The balanced scorecard measures IT across several perspectives, such as contribution to the business, users, internal processes and future readiness, not only cost."),
("In COBIT 2019, which set of activities belongs to governance rather than management?",
 "Evaluate, direct and monitor", ["Plan, build and run", "Deliver, service and support", "Align, plan and organize"],
 "COBIT separates governance, the Evaluate, Direct and Monitor domain owned by the board, from the management domains that plan, build, run and monitor IT."),
("Which document is most likely to need updating when a team adopts a new backup tool?",
 "The backup procedure", ["The information security policy", "The IT strategic plan", "The audit committee charter"],
 "Procedures describe how tasks are done with specific tools, so they change most often. Policies state management's intent and should rarely need to change for a tool."),
("When reviewing IT policies, what should an IS auditor check first?",
 "That they are approved and communicated", ["That each policy fits on a single page", "That IT staff wrote every policy", "That policies name specific products"],
 "A policy has force only when senior management has approved it and staff know about it. Content and format are reviewed after that."),
("What is the main advantage of developing policies top-down rather than bottom-up?",
 "They stay consistent with strategy", ["They are always quicker to write", "They need no management review", "They cover only day-to-day operational detail"],
 "Top-down policies start from organizational objectives, so they are consistent across the enterprise. Bottom-up policies can be practical but may conflict or leave gaps."),
("Which situation is the greatest segregation of duties concern?",
 "A developer promoting own code", ["A help desk analyst resetting passwords", "An operator scheduling batch jobs", "A tester writing test scripts"],
 "If the person who writes code also moves it into production, unauthorized or untested changes can go live with no one else seeing them."),
("In NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0, what do the Tiers (Partial, Risk Informed, Repeatable, Adaptive) describe?",
 "How rigorous risk governance is", ["Which controls a vendor must buy", "How sensitive each data set is", "The order of incident response steps"],
 "CSF 2.0 Tiers characterize the rigor of an organization's cybersecurity risk governance and management practices. Profiles, not Tiers, describe current and target outcomes."),
("A bank outsources its data center to a provider. Who remains accountable for the security of customer data?",
 "The bank", ["The provider", "The provider's auditor", "The regulator"],
 "Responsibility for operations can be outsourced, but accountability cannot. The bank must oversee the provider through the contract and monitoring."),
("Which contract clause matters most to an IS auditor reviewing a new cloud service agreement?",
 "A right to audit or receive assurance", ["A discount for paying three years upfront", "A clause naming the provider's office", "A clause on the provider's marketing rights"],
 "Without a right to audit, or to independent assurance reports, the organization cannot check that the provider's controls meet its requirements."),
("A provider keeps missing the service levels in its contract. Which contract term gives the customer a direct remedy?",
 "Service credits or penalties", ["A clause naming the account manager", "A list of the provider's other clients", "A description of the provider's history"],
 "Service credits or penalties tied to each service level give the provider a financial reason to perform and the customer a remedy short of ending the contract. Repeated misses can also trigger termination rights."),
("Before signing with a new critical IT supplier, what should be assessed first?",
 "Its financial health and controls", ["Its marketing material and brand logo", "The number of offices it has", "The size of its sales team"],
 "Due diligence checks that the supplier can keep delivering and will protect the data. A supplier that fails financially or has weak controls becomes the customer's problem."),
("IT staff grant access to customer data on request, without business sign-off. What is the main concern?",
 "The data owner never approves access", ["Access is granted too slowly", "The requests are sent by email, not tickets", "IT staff work too many hours"],
 "The business owner of the data should decide who may use it. When IT grants access on its own, there is no assurance access matches business need."),
("What is the main purpose of enterprise architecture?",
 "To align IT with business goals", ["To document the network cabling layout", "To choose the cheapest hardware", "To set IT staff pay grades"],
 "Enterprise architecture describes the business, data, application and technology layers and how they fit, so that IT investments support strategy and avoid duplication."),
("The board has never stated a risk appetite. What is the main effect on IT risk management?",
 "No yardstick for accepting risk", ["Risk registers cannot be kept at all", "IT must transfer every risk it finds", "Auditors must set risk levels instead"],
 "Without an agreed appetite, managers have no consistent basis for deciding which risks to accept and which to treat, so decisions vary by person."),
("A risk remains above appetite after existing controls. What should management do?",
 "Treat it further or escalate it", ["Record it as accepted by default", "Remove it from the risk register", "Ask audit to accept the risk"],
 "Residual risk above appetite needs more treatment, such as new controls or transfer, or a formal decision by someone with authority. Auditors never accept risk for management."),
("Each entry in a risk register should name a risk owner. Why?",
 "So someone answers for the response", ["So the auditor can contact the vendor", "So the risk can be deleted later", "So costs can be billed to IT"],
 "A named owner with authority makes sure the risk is monitored and the agreed treatment is carried out. Unowned risks tend to be forgotten."),
("When should a privacy impact assessment be carried out for a new system?",
 "Before it processes personal data", ["After the first year of live operation", "Only after a data breach occurs", "When the system is retired"],
 "Assessing privacy risk early lets controls be built in by design. Doing it after go-live means expensive rework or living with avoidable risk."),
("A web form asks customers for their date of birth, which the service never uses. Which privacy principle does this break?",
 "Data minimization", ["Data portability", "Accountability", "Storage limitation"],
 "Data minimization means collecting only the personal data needed for the stated purpose. Unused fields add risk with no benefit."),
("What is the most effective long-term way to improve data quality across the organization?",
 "Assign data owners and stewards", ["Run a one-off data cleanup project", "Buy a larger database server", "Restrict reports to IT staff"],
 "Data governance gives named people responsibility for defining and maintaining data quality. One-off cleanups fix today's errors but not their causes."),
("Which measure shows whether an IT goal has been achieved, after the fact?",
 "A key goal indicator", ["A key performance indicator", "A key risk indicator", "A control self-assessment"],
 "Key goal indicators are lagging measures of outcomes. Key performance indicators are leading measures of how well processes are performing toward the goal."),
("What is the main purpose of benchmarking IT performance against peers?",
 "To find where processes can improve", ["To set the external audit fee each year", "To meet a regulatory filing", "To choose the board members"],
 "Benchmarking compares the organization's processes and costs with others to find gaps and good practices worth adopting."),
("An organization charges each business unit for the IT services it uses. What is the main benefit?",
 "Units answer for their IT use", ["IT no longer needs a budget", "Security costs go to zero", "Projects need no business case"],
 "Chargeback links consumption to cost, which encourages business units to use IT resources sensibly and helps justify investment."),
("Where should the IT quality assurance function sit to be most effective?",
 "Independent of the development team", ["Inside the development team it checks", "Under the project manager", "Within the vendor's staff"],
 "Quality assurance needs to judge work objectively. If it reports to the people whose work it checks, problems may be played down."),
("An IS auditor finds that leavers' accounts often stay active for weeks because HR emails IT late. What is the best fix?",
 "An automated HR feed to deprovisioning", ["A reminder poster in the HR office", "A yearly review of all user accounts", "Longer passwords for every account"],
 "Linking the HR leaver record directly to account removal takes the manual hand-off out of the process, so access ends on the leaving date. A yearly review would find the accounts far too late."),
("What is the main purpose of a non-disclosure agreement signed when staff join?",
 "To bind them to confidentiality", ["To list their job duties", "To set their pay, benefits and leave", "To record their training"],
 "A non-disclosure agreement creates a legal obligation not to reveal confidential information, during and after employment."),
("How can an organization best show it manages the laws and regulations that apply to its IT?",
 "A current register with named owners", ["A single copy of each law in the library", "An annual email reminder to staff", "A clause in every vendor contract"],
 "A register of applicable requirements, kept current and with an owner for each, lets the organization show it knows its obligations and tracks compliance."),
("What is the most effective way to detect unlicensed software across the estate?",
 "Reconcile installs to license records", ["Ask users to declare all their own software", "Read each vendor's license terms", "Review the IT purchasing budget"],
 "An automated inventory of installed software reconciled to purchase and license records finds installs with no license behind them."),
("Contractors are given network accounts for a six-month project. Which control matters most?",
 "Accounts that expire with the contract", ["Accounts with no password set", "Shared accounts for the whole project team", "Accounts reviewed every five years"],
 "Setting an expiry date tied to the contract ensures access ends when the work does, even if no one remembers to request removal."),
("What is the main benefit of IT portfolio management?",
 "It ranks investments by value and risk", ["It removes the need for budgets", "It approves every project request it receives", "It replaces project management"],
 "Portfolio management looks at all proposed and current investments together so limited funds go to those with the best value for acceptable risk."),
("Business units have bought cloud tools without IT's knowledge. What is the main governance concern?",
 "Data sits outside known controls", ["IT staff will have less work to do", "The tools may look different", "Users may need new passwords"],
 "Shadow IT puts organizational data in services the organization has not assessed, so security, privacy and continuity risks go unmanaged."),
("Why do many organizations have the CISO report outside the IT operations line?",
 "To keep security oversight independent", ["To cut the security team's budget", "To avoid writing security policies entirely", "To let security run the help desk"],
 "If security reports to the head of the area it oversees, findings may be traded off against delivery goals. A separate line reduces that conflict."),
("What is the most important factor in a successful IT governance program?",
 "Visible support from the top", ["The newest governance software", "A large IT budget surplus", "A long list of new policies"],
 "Governance depends on leadership setting direction and holding people to account. Tools and policies help, but without support from the top they are ignored."),
("A key supplier relies on a subcontractor to host the organization's data. What should the organization do?",
 "Require oversight of the subcontractor", ["Assume the subcontractor is out of scope entirely", "Contact the subcontractor's customers", "Ask the supplier to drop the subcontractor"],
 "Risk passes down the supply chain. Contracts should require the supplier to manage subcontractors to the same standard and to report on them."),
]
